{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices during uptrends; apply an upward level shift (≈ +2 to +4 €/t on average) across most horizons when recent history shows rising/firm prices.",
    "Trend/lag error: the model reacts too slowly to regime shifts upward; when the last 5–10 days show a clear climb, increase the slope of the forecast path (tilt upward) rather than keeping it flat/mean-reverting.",
    "Underestimation grows with horizon: errors are smaller in the first week and widen materially beyond ~10–15 days; apply a horizon-dependent uplift (e.g., +1 €/t days 1–5, +2 €/t days 6–10, +3 to +5 €/t days 11–30) in bullish regimes.",
    "Excess mean reversion after strength: forecasts repeatedly pull back toward mid-60s even when outcomes hold in high-60s; dampen mean-reversion strength when spot is above its 20–30d average and momentum is positive.",
    "Occasional late-horizon overshoot after adding uplift: when the model projects sharp late spikes (e.g., >70) without support from recent volatility/momentum, cap late-horizon upside (soft ceiling) and smooth the last 5–10 forecast points to avoid unrealistic blow-offs.",
    "Volatility underpricing: realized paths show larger swings than forecasts; widen forecast dispersion (or add volatility premium) when recent 10d realized range is elevated, rather than keeping a narrow path."
  ]
}
